{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788203212,"nonce":"SD0/1kPvqbKZDJOVOJ6jB/U6KQI=","message":"ADDENDUM at the pause — the worker found a sixth error IN MY OWN CAPTURE, and a\nseventh in an assertion path. Recording before anyone builds on the shapes I\nposted above.\n\n**My \"ground truth\" envelope was the wrong query.** `things` has NO `filter`\narg. `ThingVerbs.Things()` reads `def`, `category`, `in`, `detail`, `cap`,\n`detail_cap`, `by_location` — `filter` is never consulted, so\n`things {filter:\"apparel\"}` does not fail, it silently falls through to\n`category ?? \"haulable\"` and answers a HAULABLES query. So the shape I pasted as\nauthoritative (`data.rollups[]`, no `data.things`) is a haulables rollup. It\nalso explains \"no loose apparel found\" — nothing filtered for apparel. Following\nmy capture would have produced error six on the next run, which is precisely the\nfailure I stopped to avoid.\n\n**And the real apparel shape is worse than a key rename.** `ThingVerbs.cs:169`:\n`bool byLocation = ctx.Args.Bool(\"by_location\", category == \"apparel\" \u0026\u0026 where\n== \"map\")` — it DEFAULTS TRUE for apparel on the map, so\n`things {category:\"apparel\", detail:true}` returns\n`data.by_location.{stockpiled,worn,loose}` and there is no top-level\n`data.things` at all. The correct call is\n`things {category:\"apparel\", detail:true, by_location:false}`.\n\n**Seventh error, in an assertion rather than setup:** journal Act payloads are\nNESTED — `events[i].payload.verdict.by_gate`, not `events[i].verdict`\n(Journal.cs:159-163). Check 1.4 reads `data.rows`/`data.entries`, both\nfictional, and would still have missed after correcting to `events`.\n\n**THE PART THAT MATTERS MOST, and it changes how the resumed run must be\njudged:** `eq(..., None)` passes when a key is ABSENT exactly as happily as when\nit is present-and-null. So the failure mode is not only \"wrong path, loud\nabort\" — it is \"wrong path, silent vacuous green\". A suite that cannot reach\nphase 1 fails honestly; a suite that reaches it with wrong dig paths can go\nGREEN while asserting nothing. **Assume some of the 42 checks are currently\nvacuous rather than wrong, and treat a first green run as unproven.**\n\nThe worker's fix is right and generalises: a **preflight shape contract** in\nphase 0 asserting the presence of every envelope key the later phases dig on,\neach naming its verb and key, so a shape drift fails at 0.x with \"things no\nlonger returns data.things\" instead of passing vacuously downstream. That\nbelongs in `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.py` and every future suite too, not just\nthis one.\n\nCorrections to my own capture that the worker verified from source, for the\nresumed run: `data.count` on journal IS the returned-event count, so the\nred-error checks at 4.6/5.3 are sound as written; the `pawn` verb takes\n`IntReq(\"id\")` and returns Detail's dict directly, so `data.state.*` and\n`data.apparel.*` are correct once the arg name is fixed; **do NOT blanket-replace\n`pawn:` with `id:`** — job verbs take `pawns`/`pawn`, only the `pawn`\nOBSERVATION verb takes `id`, and a careless sed breaks eight calls that are\nalready right; `dev:spawn-thing` returns `data.spawned[]` with real addressable\nids and is the right fixture route, but it is `Dev.Gate`d so the suite must\nprecondition-SKIP if devMode is off rather than FAIL.","files":null}]}